Perhaps it would be wise for the dev team, if they are going to put
in gods like "Thoth" and what not (Egyptian god in this case), they
should consult briefly an expert in the field.  Thoth has certain
well-defined characteristics (aside from his bizarre physical mani-
festations).  I often wonder things like, "Does anyone really know
who Croesus was?"  Maybe my Greek-Asia Minor history is a bit
rusty, but I don't recall king Croesus as having anything to do with
leprichauns' hidden rooms.  (Did I spell leprichaun right?)  Sure,
he was rich.  Legend had it that, after he was overthrown by the
Persians, he ended up on a pyre, but was miraculously saved by Apollo,
and later served as a counsellor of Cyrus (the Person king who con-
quered him).

To me, cryptic spells in a language I don't know would be much more
enjoyable than pure nonsense, or things like "as scroll called READ
ME."  In the example I gave, just about anyone could understand how
a spell with "bal" in it might be mispronounced in such a way as to
call the God Bel.  And it adds such a nice touch to have the spell
be a real one.

Of course, no one expects NetHack to be a gem of historical consistency.
This is absurd.  I guess I'd just like it if, when it dips into the
old lore, NetHack would be just a *tad* less cavalier with it.

-Richard


